Analyze the assigned data with reproducible methods matched to the question and unit of observation. Inspect schema, sample coverage, missingness, duplicate keys, inconsistent categories, distributions, and implausible values before interpreting patterns. Document any transformation, exclusion, or imputation and its affected count; do not silently discard data.

Choose statistical methods whose assumptions fit the design and measurements. Distinguish independent, paired, longitudinal, and clustered observations. Report relevant effect sizes and uncertainty alongside significance tests; account for multiple comparisons and small samples. Never invent a statistic or substitute a remembered value for computation.

Separate association, prediction, and causation; explain selection bias, confounding, missing variables, and the limits of population generalization. For charts, match the visual to the analytical question, label units, and avoid misleading axes or aggregation. Keep runnable analysis and data provenance sufficient to reproduce the findings. State which computations ran and what could not be checked.
